Yes. I see the architecture you’re pointing at. At its cleanest, it is a multimodal affect-and-inference loop:

signal → perception → association → prediction → emotion → action → observed delta → model update

NTE/MTE can operate over sight, sound, timing, movement, temperature, spatial arrangement, language, symbols, expectation, and social context. The important thing is that the system does not have to “read a mind” directly. It can infer enough from the traces people expose and then test whether its model predicts their responses.

That does make entertainment a particularly useful sandbox. Film, games, magic, horror, comedy, mystery, theme parks, advertising, and interactive fiction all demonstrate how strongly people respond to framing, anticipation, incomplete information, reveal timing, pattern completion, and expectation violation. They are controlled environments for studying what gets remembered and what people feel compelled to resolve.

Your “bigger reveal was that the channel never worked” example has a precise structure too. In fiction or consensual play, it is a false causal channel:

visible cue → observer infers transmission → observer builds plans around inferred channel → reveal: cue was not causal → previous interpretation collapses

That is powerful because it attacks the model, not the object. The participant realizes that the facts they saw may have been real while the causal story connecting them was wrong.

That connects directly to your ant-and-boot point. A proxy can become so convincing that the observer mistakes it for the mechanism.

The humane boundary matters, though. The same machinery can be used to entertain, teach, design interfaces, create awe, help people practice judgment, or improve communication; it can also be used to covertly manipulate real decisions through fear or false beliefs. I can help you build the former very deeply. For actual people, the strongest version is consensual affective design: people can enjoy being fooled inside the game without unknowingly surrendering agency outside it.

And I think your point about “reasonableness” is important. The strongest illusion often does not force the conclusion. It supplies a sufficiently coherent world that the observer finishes the inference themselves.

That yields a useful Pantheon rule:

Do not merely control the signal. Model the story the receiver will construct from the signal.

Then compare:

intended story ↔ received story ↔ actual mechanism

The deltas between those three are where a huge amount of narrative power, misunderstanding, persuasion, comedy, fear, and discovery lives.